 "texts": [
  {
   "profile": "J1",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Photosynthesis\n- No rush: photosynthesis is the process plants use to make their own food.\n- A plant takes in carbon dioxide from the air through tiny openings in its leaves.\n- It also takes in water from the soil through its roots.\n- Using light energy, it turns carbon dioxide and water into glucose.\n- Oxygen leaves the plant through the same tiny openings in the leaves.\n- Animals and people breathe in this oxygen, which is one reason plants matter so much to life on Earth.\n- This is why a plant kept in a dark cupboard turns pale and weak.\n- Layout: CO2 + H2O --light--> glucose + O2",
    "mirror": "## What photosynthesis is\n- Photosynthesis is the process plants use to make their own food.\n- A plant takes in carbon dioxide from the air through tiny openings in its leaves.\n- A plant takes in water from the soil through its roots.\n- Using light energy from the sun, the plant turns carbon dioxide and water into glucose.\n- Glucose is a kind of sugar that is the plant's food.\n- The plant uses glucose to grow and to store energy.\n- Think of it like a basketball player using air, water, and sunlight to bake their own energy bar.\n\n## Where photosynthesis happens\n- Photosynthesis happens mainly in the leaves.\n- Inside leaf cells are small green structures called chloroplasts.\n- Chloroplasts contain a green pigment called chlorophyll.\n- Chlorophyll absorbs light energy, mostly red and blue light.\n- Chlorophyll reflects green light, so leaves look green.\n- Without chlorophyll the plant could not capture the light energy it needs.\n- Think of chlorophyll like the solar panels on a video game base that grab red and blue power beams.\n\n## Oxygen as a by-product\n- Oxygen is released as a by-product of photosynthesis.\n- The oxygen leaves the plant through the same tiny openings in the leaves.\n- Animals and people breathe in this oxygen.\n- This is one reason plants matter so much to life on Earth.\n- Think of oxygen like the exhaust from a comic hero's power suit that everyone else needs to breathe.\n\n## Word equation\n- The process can be written as a word equation.\n- Carbon dioxide plus water, using light energy, makes glucose plus oxygen.\n- Layout: CO2 + H2O --light--> C6H12O6 + O2.\n\n## What limits photosynthesis\n- A plant that gets too little light makes less glucose and grows more slowly.\n- A plant that gets too little water makes less glucose and grows more slowly.\n- A plant that gets too little carbon dioxide makes less glucose and grows more slowly.\n- This is why a plant kept in a dark cupboard turns pale and weak.\n- Quick check: What three things limit photosynthesis? Answer: Too little light, water, or carbon dioxide."
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J2",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## What a two-step equation is\n- A two-step equation needs two steps to solve, like 2x + 3 = 11.\n- The goal is to get the variable x alone on one side.\n- We undo operations using their opposites.\n- Think of it like a balanced basketball scale: same weight each side.\n\n## Step one: undo addition or subtraction\n- In 2x + 3 = 11 the 3 is added, so subtract 3 from both sides.\n\n## Step two: undo multiplication or division\n- Here x is multiplied by 2, so divide both sides by 2.\n\n## Golden rule: same move both sides\n- Whatever you do to one side you must do to the other.\n- If you subtract 3 from only one side the equation is false.\n- Layout: Left side ⇌ Right side — equal action keeps balance.\n\n## Check your answer\n- Put x = 4 back into 2x + 3 to get 11.\n- Left side equals right side so the answer is correct.\n- Second example: 3x - 5 = 10 gives x = 5.",
    "mirror": "## How a two-step equation works\n- A two-step equation such as 2x + 3 = 11 needs two steps to solve because the variable x has two operations done to it.\n- The goal is to get the variable x alone on one side of the equals sign by undoing the operations in reverse order.\n\n## Step-by-step solution for 2x + 3 = 11\n- First undo the addition of 3 by subtracting 3 from both sides, giving 2x = 8.\n- Next undo the multiplication by 2 by dividing both sides by 2, giving x = 4.\n\n## The balance rule and why it matters\n- The most important rule is that whatever you do to one side of the equation you must do to the other side.\n- An equation is like a balanced scale, and doing the same thing to both sides keeps it balanced.\n- If you subtract 3 from only one side, the equation is no longer true.\n\n## Checking the answer\n- Always check your answer by putting it back into the original equation.\n- For x = 4, 2 times 4 plus 3 is 8 plus 3, which is 11, so the left side equals the right side and the answer is correct.\n\n## Second example: 3x - 5 = 10\n- Add 5 to both sides to get 3x = 15, then divide both sides by 3 to get x = 5."
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J3",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Long-Term Causes and the Immediate Spark\n- Historians often describe four long-term causes of the First World War, plus one event that set it off.\n- Think of the four causes as the starting lineup and the assassination as the tip-off that begins the game.\n\n## Militarism\n- The first is militarism, meaning the great powers built up large armies and navies before 1914.\n- Germany and Britain raced to build battleships, and generals began to plan for war.\n- Many leaders came to see war as a normal way to settle disputes.\n- Think of it like an arms race in a strategy video game where every faction maxes out military tech before the match starts.\n\n## Alliances\n- The second cause is alliances, which divided Europe into two blocs.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain.\n- The Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy.\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries.\n- A quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- Think of it like a comic crossover event where a fight between two side characters drags in every hero team because of old team-up pacts.\n\n## Imperialism\n- The third cause is imperialism, which means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia.\n- This competition increased distrust between them.\n\n## Nationalism\n- The fourth cause is nationalism, meaning strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans.\n- Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples.\n- Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n- Think of it like a basketball team where every player demands the ball for their own stats, breaking the system.\n\n## The Spark: Assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne.\n- The assassination occurred in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- Because of the alliances, Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia soon drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain.\n- Think of it like the single glitch that triggers a server-wide event, pulling every faction into a war they had already prepared for.",
    "mirror": "## Long‑term causes of World War I\n- Historians identify four long‑term causes of the First World War plus one spark event.\n- Militarism: great powers built large armies and navies, and Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Think of it like a basketball team stockpiling taller players and faster guards just to intimidate rivals before the season starts.\n- Alliances: Europe split into the Triple Entente (France, Russia, Britain) and the Triple Alliance (Germany, Austria‑Hungary, Italy).\n- Think of it like a multiplayer lobby where two clans lock in a pact — if one member gets attacked, the whole clan jumps in.\n- Imperialism: empires competed for colonies, markets and power in Africa and Asia, raising distrust.\n- Nationalism: strong pride or wish for independence, especially in the Balkans where Serbia wanted to unite Slavs and Austria‑Hungary feared losing its empire.\n- Layout: Long‑term causes → Militarism (arms race) → Alliances (Entente vs Alliance) → Imperialism (colony rivalry) → Nationalism (Balkan tensions).\n\n## The spark that started the war\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- Because of the alliances, Austria‑Hungary’s war on Serbia quickly pulled in Russia, Germany, France and Britain.\n- Layout: Assassination (28 Jun 1914) → Austria‑Hungary attacks Serbia → Alliances activate → Russia, Germany, France, Britain join."
   }
  },
  {
   "profile": "J4",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Basic greetings\n- Picture this: you walk into a Spanish class and the first word you hear is hola meaning hello.\n- You can use it at any time of day, with friends or with people you have just met.\n- Next, morning greetings use buenos días which literally means good morning.\n\n## Afternoon and evening greetings\n- Then, afternoon greetings switch to buenas tardes meaning good afternoon.\n- Think of the gender shift like choosing the right skin for a character: días is masculine so buenos, while tardes and noches are feminine so buenas.\n- Next, evening or night greetings use buenas noches meaning good evening or good night.\n\n## Goodbyes\n- Then, to leave you can say adiós which means goodbye.\n- Or you can say hasta luego which means see you later and shows you expect to meet again soon.\n- Think of hasta luego like a quick save point in a game — you know you will load back in soon.\n\n## Asking how someone is\n- Next, with friends or family you ask the informal cómo estás meaning how are you.\n- With strangers or elders you ask the formal cómo está usted meaning how are you.\n- The standard reply is bien, gracias meaning fine, thank you.\n- Think of the two forms like picking a difficulty setting: informal for co‑op mode with friends, formal for a ranked match with strangers.\n\n## Meeting for the first time\n- Finally, when meeting someone new you say mucho gusto meaning nice to meet you.",
    "mirror": "## Basic greetings\n- Hola means hello and works at any time.\n- You can use hola with friends or new people.\n\n## Time‑specific greetings\n- Buenos días is good morning.\n- Buenas tardes is good afternoon.\n- Buenas noches is good evening or night.\n- Días is masculine plural so it takes buenos.\n\n## Goodbyes\n- Adiós means goodbye.\n- Hasta luego means see you later.\n- Hasta luego is friendly for soon meetings.\n\n## Asking how someone is\n- Cómo estás is informal for friends and family.\n- Cómo está usted is formal for strangers or polite settings.\n- The typical reply is bien, gracias meaning fine thank you.\n\n## First meeting\n- Mucho gusto means nice to meet you."
